(Step 4: Inverse-MAE blender) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 716.00 Tm
(The two model outputs meet in the blender. Inverse MAE means the model with lower error gets) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 701.00 Tm
(more weight. So if CatBoost has been more accurate, it counts more. If the Transformer is) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 686.00 Tm
(better for a player, it gets more weight instead.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 671.00 Tm
( This keeps the final answer stable instead of trusting only one model.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 656.00 Tm
( MAE stands for mean absolute error, which is a simple measure of past mistakes.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 641.00 Tm
( The blender is the merge point in the diagram.) Tj
/F2 15 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 622.00 Tm
(Step 5: Monte Carlo simulation) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 600.00 Tm
(After blending, the program does many simulations. This is how it turns one estimate into a) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 585.00 Tm
(range of outcomes. The simulator keeps the stat groups linked together so the results look) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54.00 570.00 Tm
(realistic.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 555.00 Tm
( One game is simulated many times, not just once.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 540.00 Tm
( Points, rebounds, assists, steals, blocks, and turnovers move together in a realistic) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 82.00 525.00 Tm
(way.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 70.00 510.00 Tm
( This makes the final projection more useful than a single raw prediction.) Tj
